<article class="env-proposition" id="03Q8">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03Q8">Proposition <span data-tag="03Q8">4.9.1.14</span>.</a> Let $(S, \leq )$ and $(T, \leq )$ be linearly ordered sets, and let $f,f': S \hookrightarrow T$ be strictly increasing functions. Suppose that $S$ is well-ordered and that $f$ is an initial segment embedding. Then, for each $s \in S$, we have $f(s) \leq f'(s)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $S_0 = \{  s \in S: f'(s) &lt; f(s) \} $. We wish to show that $S_0$ is empty. Assume otherwise. Since $S$ is well-ordered, there is a least element $s \in S_0$. Since $f$ is an initial segment embedding, the inequality $f'(s) &lt; f(s)$ implies that we can write $f'(s) = f(t)$ for some $t &lt; s$. Then $t \notin S_0$, so we must have $f(t) \leq f'(t)$. It follows that $f'(s) \leq f'(t)$, contradicting our assumption that the function $f'$ is strictly increasing. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
